WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.400
Imagine a factory that just suddenly upgrades its entire assembly line to pump out a thousand cars a day.

00:07.920 --> 00:10.200
Like a massive overnight industrial boom.

00:10.340 --> 00:12.980
Right. And the factory owners are just thrilled.

00:13.340 --> 00:17.240
I mean, they're taking photos, they're issuing these breathless press releases,

00:17.240 --> 00:20.160
talking about how they've completely revolutionized manufacturing.

00:20.460 --> 00:22.100
It sounds like a total success story.

00:22.400 --> 00:24.460
Well, yeah, but there's a catch.

00:24.580 --> 00:25.380
And it's a big one.

00:25.380 --> 00:31.280
The town where this factory is located, it still only has three automotive safety inspectors.

00:31.480 --> 00:33.840
Oh, man. That is not enough.

00:34.080 --> 00:37.880
Exactly. So the factory is just churning out this massive mountain of vehicles,

00:38.000 --> 00:40.700
parking all over the streets, on the sidewalks, everywhere.

00:40.940 --> 00:41.220
Right.

00:41.540 --> 00:46.660
And the inspectors are just standing there looking up at this metallic avalanche,

00:46.740 --> 00:51.720
knowing they are legally and ethically required to check every single brake line by hand

00:51.720 --> 00:53.480
before anyone can actually drive them.

00:53.480 --> 00:55.480
It's just a paralyzing bottleneck.

00:55.840 --> 00:57.240
I mean, the system completely breaks.

00:57.500 --> 01:01.360
Yes. And the factory owners aren't paying for those extra inspectors.

01:01.640 --> 01:02.220
Of course not.

01:02.340 --> 01:02.440
No.

01:02.740 --> 01:05.360
They are just dumping the output and, you know,

01:05.440 --> 01:08.960
expecting the public infrastructure to somehow handle all the quality control.

01:09.220 --> 01:13.300
And that dynamic, that exact collision between hyper-automated production

01:13.300 --> 01:18.420
and this stubbornly human verification, well, that is the subject of our deep dive today.

01:18.900 --> 01:20.480
It's such a crucial topic right now.

01:20.520 --> 01:21.040
It really is.

01:21.040 --> 01:26.460
We are looking at a brand new essay published literally this month, November 2026,

01:26.680 --> 01:29.540
by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

01:30.080 --> 01:30.300
Yeah.

01:30.400 --> 01:36.280
The essay is titled Corporate Authorship and Verification Externalities in Automated Mathematical Research.

01:36.620 --> 01:39.500
It's a bit of a mouthful, but the content is just incredible.

01:39.720 --> 01:41.960
It is a truly remarkable piece of scholarship.

01:42.480 --> 01:47.300
I mean, what Flyxion has done is essentially write the definitive autopsy of an ongoing crisis

01:47.300 --> 01:48.580
in the scientific community.

01:48.580 --> 01:52.600
An autopsy is a good word for it because it really cuts through the noise.

01:52.780 --> 01:53.060
It does.

01:53.640 --> 01:57.720
It strips away all the breathless hype about artificial intelligence solving these grand

01:57.720 --> 01:58.520
math problems.

01:58.700 --> 02:04.880
And it forces us to look at the hidden, completely uncompensated human labor that props the whole

02:04.880 --> 02:05.520
illusion up.

02:05.720 --> 02:05.900
Right.

02:05.900 --> 02:09.860
Because if you're just casually scrolling and reading the headlines, the story is always,

02:10.040 --> 02:12.760
you know, AI solves 50-year-old math mystery.

02:13.380 --> 02:14.020
Which sounds amazing.

02:14.240 --> 02:15.480
It sounds like the future has arrived.

02:15.840 --> 02:20.560
But if you actually dig into this essay, the reality is a lot messier.

02:20.800 --> 02:24.660
And honestly, it's a lot more concerning for the future of how we produce knowledge as a

02:24.660 --> 02:24.980
species.

02:25.540 --> 02:25.900
Absolutely.

02:25.900 --> 02:28.660
And this isn't just about abstract math.

02:29.020 --> 02:29.400
Not at all.

02:29.720 --> 02:35.820
Whether you're a lawyer staring down AI-generated contracts or a doctor looking at AI-generated

02:35.820 --> 02:40.720
diagnostics or just, you know, someone trying to figure out what is actually true on the

02:40.720 --> 02:41.320
internet today.

02:41.700 --> 02:44.560
The mechanics of this crisis, they apply to you.

02:44.880 --> 02:45.720
They apply to everyone.

02:46.580 --> 02:51.800
So to start, we really need to understand the sheer unprecedented scale of what happened

02:51.800 --> 02:53.580
over the last few months of 2026.

02:53.780 --> 02:57.620
Because this wasn't just some tech company releasing a cool new calculator app.

02:58.080 --> 02:58.560
Not at all.

02:58.960 --> 03:02.120
To understand the gravity of Flyxion's critique, we have to look at the timeline.

03:02.540 --> 03:03.260
Walk us through it.

03:03.700 --> 03:09.280
So the essay meticulously tracks a sequence of releases from OpenAI that basically acted

03:09.280 --> 03:12.960
as a massive stress test for the entire mathematical community.

03:13.100 --> 03:14.220
Like a shock to the system.

03:14.440 --> 03:14.800
Exactly.

03:14.800 --> 03:20.520
It started on August 1st, 2026, when they released 10 significant mathematical results.

03:21.160 --> 03:25.600
But the defining characteristic of that first release wasn't really the math itself.

03:25.960 --> 03:26.420
What was it?

03:26.720 --> 03:27.680
It was the byline.

03:28.200 --> 03:31.240
The work was credited entirely to the corporate organization.

03:31.560 --> 03:33.820
There were absolutely no human authors listed.

03:34.180 --> 03:38.920
Just this monolithic corporate entity basically claiming to have birthed pure mathematics out

03:38.920 --> 03:39.380
of nowhere.

03:39.660 --> 03:40.220
Precisely.

03:40.700 --> 03:41.840
Just OpenAI.

03:41.840 --> 03:44.700
And that was really just the warning shot.

03:44.800 --> 03:45.140
Right.

03:45.320 --> 03:46.600
Because then came October.

03:46.960 --> 03:47.160
Right.

03:47.680 --> 03:49.580
Fast forward to October 2026.

03:49.780 --> 03:54.280
And they released a repository containing 722 manuscripts.

03:55.160 --> 03:55.680
722.

03:55.680 --> 03:56.120
Yeah.

03:56.320 --> 03:59.300
Organized into 372 related result families.

03:59.300 --> 04:06.420
And then literally, just yesterday, November 24th, 2026, they dropped another major preprint.

04:06.420 --> 04:07.800
And this one was a big deal.

04:07.800 --> 04:09.520
Oh, a massive deal.

04:09.980 --> 04:13.700
This one was targeting a legendary white whale in computer science.

04:13.700 --> 04:16.820
That's the exact exponent of matrix multiplication.

04:16.820 --> 04:17.320
Okay.

04:17.440 --> 04:19.460
Let's just pause on that number for a second.

04:21.420 --> 04:23.720
722 manuscripts in a single drop.

04:23.800 --> 04:25.820
It's hard to even comprehend that volume.

04:26.080 --> 04:30.800
If you are, say, a graduate student, writing one solid manuscript can take a year.

04:31.260 --> 04:31.900
Sometimes more.

04:32.180 --> 04:32.540
Easily.

04:32.540 --> 04:37.880
And even a highly productive tenured professor might publish, what, a few papers a year?

04:38.220 --> 04:39.860
If they're working constantly, sure.

04:40.140 --> 04:44.780
So dropping over 700 at once, I mean, it's basically a denial of service attack on the

04:44.780 --> 04:45.620
peer review system.

04:45.940 --> 04:50.300
That is the exact mathematical reality Flyxion models later in the essay.

04:50.700 --> 04:54.880
But to understand why this volume breaks the system so badly, the essay first breaks down

04:54.880 --> 04:57.580
how mathematical research actually works in the real world.

04:57.580 --> 04:59.000
Okay, let's unpack this.

04:59.320 --> 05:01.100
Because I think people just assume math is math.

05:01.360 --> 05:01.560
Right.

05:02.460 --> 05:07.380
But Flyxion identifies five conceptual steps that actually constitute mathematical research.

05:08.200 --> 05:12.060
Historically, these were all bundled together in the mind of a single human mathematician.

05:12.600 --> 05:14.200
But now they're being pulled apart.

05:14.660 --> 05:16.380
Violently unbundled, yes.

05:16.980 --> 05:17.980
So what are the five steps?

05:18.400 --> 05:20.640
Step one is conjecture formation.

05:21.300 --> 05:22.340
This is the creative leap.

05:22.480 --> 05:26.580
It's guessing what might be true based on intuition and recognizing patterns.

05:26.580 --> 05:28.820
Like looking at the stars and guessing how they move.

05:29.080 --> 05:29.900
Sure, exactly.

05:31.020 --> 05:32.700
Then step two is proof construction.

05:33.380 --> 05:37.880
This is building the rigorous logical bridge from your basic axioms to the conjecture to

05:37.880 --> 05:39.660
show that it is unequivocally true.

05:39.920 --> 05:44.620
So having the big idea and then doing the grueling scratch pad work to actually prove it.

05:44.960 --> 05:45.260
Yes.

05:45.960 --> 05:47.560
Now step three is verification.

05:48.300 --> 05:52.500
This is having another competent mind check every single plank on that logical bridge you

05:52.500 --> 05:52.980
just built.

05:53.380 --> 05:55.080
To make sure it doesn't collapse under scrutiny.

05:55.580 --> 05:56.060
Exactly.

05:56.580 --> 05:58.000
You need an independent auditor.

05:58.660 --> 06:00.040
Step four is exposition.

06:00.040 --> 06:05.580
This is taking that highly technical, dense proof and writing it up in a way that the

06:05.580 --> 06:09.180
broader community can actually understand, internalize, and build upon.

06:09.800 --> 06:12.020
Because if no one can read it, it doesn't really matter.

06:12.240 --> 06:12.380
Right.

06:13.100 --> 06:15.240
And finally, step five is attribution.

06:15.700 --> 06:19.500
Accurately recording who contributed what to the entire process.

06:19.500 --> 06:25.740
Okay, so conjecture, proof, verification, exposition, attribution.

06:26.280 --> 06:26.860
Five steps.

06:27.260 --> 06:31.080
So where exactly does the AI fit into this assembly line?

06:31.920 --> 06:36.260
Well, generative AI systems have fundamentally collapsed the cost and time required for the

06:36.260 --> 06:36.960
first two steps.

06:37.240 --> 06:38.140
Just the first two?

06:38.680 --> 06:39.440
Mainly, yes.

06:39.580 --> 06:44.260
They can form conjectures and construct incredibly dense formal proofs at a scale and speed that

06:44.260 --> 06:45.640
is completely unprecedented.

06:45.640 --> 06:48.380
The machine can spit out the bridges in seconds.

06:48.720 --> 06:50.120
But what about the rest?

06:50.820 --> 06:51.920
That's the problem.

06:52.440 --> 06:58.760
The remaining three steps, verification, exposition, and attribution, those remain stubbornly inherently

06:58.760 --> 06:59.260
human.

06:59.260 --> 07:03.880
They are slow, time-intensive, labor-intensive processes.

07:04.280 --> 07:09.660
You simply cannot scale human comprehension just by adding more GPUs to a server farm in

07:09.660 --> 07:09.980
Nevada.

07:10.620 --> 07:12.680
Which brings me right back to the factory analogy.

07:13.340 --> 07:17.140
The AI is just spitting out the cars, the conjectures and the proofs.

07:17.180 --> 07:17.360
Right.

07:17.540 --> 07:21.900
But the humans still have to test drive them, write the user manuals, and figure out who actually

07:21.900 --> 07:23.600
designed the engine in the first place.

07:24.140 --> 07:28.660
And the factory owners are just pointing to this massive, overflowing lot of undriven cars

07:28.660 --> 07:31.300
and saying, look at all this transportation we created.

07:31.780 --> 07:31.800
Oh.

07:32.240 --> 07:36.780
And that tension really reaches its absolute peak with yesterday's release.

07:37.260 --> 07:37.480
Right?

07:37.580 --> 07:38.680
The one from November 24th.

07:38.700 --> 07:39.320
It really does.

07:39.720 --> 07:44.160
Because among those hundreds of manuscripts, there was one specific claim that caused the

07:44.160 --> 07:49.220
entire computer science world to basically drop their coffee and stop what they were doing.

07:49.540 --> 07:50.800
This is the white whale you mentioned.

07:50.900 --> 07:51.160
Yes.

07:51.460 --> 07:56.620
They claim to have found the exact value for the matrix multiplication exponent.

07:56.620 --> 07:59.980
Mathematicians denote this with the Greek letter omega.

08:00.440 --> 08:00.640
Okay.

08:00.800 --> 08:04.880
Let's hover here for a second because I want to make sure I'm really grasping the stakes

08:04.880 --> 08:05.240
of this.

08:06.160 --> 08:07.220
Matrix multiplication.

08:07.940 --> 08:12.520
For anyone who hasn't taken linear algebra in a while, a matrix is basically just a grid

08:12.520 --> 08:12.920
of numbers.

08:12.920 --> 08:13.940
That's exactly right.

08:14.160 --> 08:14.840
A simple grid.

08:15.320 --> 08:20.520
So if you're rendering a 3D video game or training a massive neural network or running

08:20.520 --> 08:25.700
a complex physics simulation, under the hood, the computer is just taking massive grids of

08:25.700 --> 08:30.080
numbers and multiplying them together over and over and over again.

08:30.260 --> 08:32.860
It is the foundational operation of modern computing.

08:33.180 --> 08:37.860
If you can find a faster way to multiply matrices, you speed up almost everything we do with computers.

08:38.200 --> 08:38.460
Period.

08:38.800 --> 08:39.020
Okay.

08:39.100 --> 08:40.340
So how do we currently do it?

08:40.920 --> 08:46.500
Historically, if you have two grids, say, each with n rows and n columns, the standard

08:46.500 --> 08:50.700
way you learn to multiply them in high school takes an amount of time proportional to n cubed.

08:51.280 --> 08:51.580
Okay.

08:51.820 --> 08:52.900
N to the power of 3.

08:53.100 --> 08:53.380
Right.

08:53.520 --> 08:53.680
Right.

08:53.680 --> 08:58.860
So if the grid gets 10 times bigger, the time it takes to multiply them gets 1,000 times

08:58.860 --> 08:59.200
longer.

08:59.620 --> 08:59.820
Oh, wow.

08:59.880 --> 09:01.180
So it scales terribly.

09:01.460 --> 09:01.860
Terribly.

09:02.020 --> 09:04.180
It gets extremely slow very fast.

09:04.180 --> 09:09.980
But in 1969, a mathematician named Volker Strassen shocked the world by proving you didn't actually

09:09.980 --> 09:11.360
need n cubed steps.

09:11.740 --> 09:12.280
He found a shortcut.

09:12.580 --> 09:18.600
He found a brilliant, very clever shortcut that took the exponent down to about 2.81.

09:19.180 --> 09:25.140
And since then, it has been this half century race to see just how low that exponent can actually

09:25.140 --> 09:25.400
go.

09:25.520 --> 09:26.480
What's the ultimate goal?

09:26.880 --> 09:28.540
Like how low could it theoretically get?

09:28.540 --> 09:34.180
The holy grail has always been to see if it can reach exactly 2, meaning the time it

09:34.180 --> 09:40.720
takes is proportional to n squared, which is the absolute physical minimum, because you

09:40.720 --> 09:45.300
have to at least read the n squared individual entries in the matrices to multiply them.

09:45.340 --> 09:47.160
Wait, you can't multiply numbers you haven't even looked at.

09:47.220 --> 09:47.580
Exactly.

09:47.920 --> 09:54.500
So it's been this creeping, agonizing progress for 50 years, down from 3 to 2.81, then what,

09:54.580 --> 09:55.940
down to 2.37 something?

09:55.940 --> 09:59.680
Every few years, someone just shaves a tiny fraction of a fraction off.

09:59.940 --> 10:01.600
Yes, it's grueling work.

10:01.760 --> 10:06.880
The previous best-known bound, achieved by researchers like Dupont and others, was hovering

10:06.880 --> 10:09.080
around 2.371.

10:09.560 --> 10:09.900
Okay.

10:10.260 --> 10:15.640
But then, the October release from the AI company showed an upper bound over complex numbers,

10:16.280 --> 10:20.640
stating that omega is actually less than or equal to nine-fourths.

10:20.780 --> 10:22.000
Nine-fourths.

10:22.020 --> 10:22.240
Yes.

10:23.020 --> 10:23.960
Which is 2.25.

10:24.300 --> 10:24.580
Yes.

10:24.580 --> 10:26.960
That is a massive jump.

10:27.540 --> 10:30.060
I mean, compared to 2.371.

10:30.160 --> 10:30.640
It's huge.

10:31.280 --> 10:34.240
But the November 24th manuscript goes even further.

10:34.620 --> 10:39.820
It claims to have established the matching lower bound for formal arithmetic circuits over

10:39.820 --> 10:40.540
every field.

10:40.680 --> 10:40.940
Wait.

10:41.520 --> 10:42.760
Matching lower bound?

10:43.180 --> 10:43.480
Yes.

10:43.960 --> 10:49.660
If the upper bound is nine-fourths, and the lower bound is nine-fourths, then they are claiming

10:49.660 --> 10:53.960
the exact fundamental value of omega is precisely nine-fourths.

10:53.960 --> 10:59.620
Okay, so on paper, this looks like the AI just dropped the mic on 50 years of human struggle.

10:59.620 --> 11:03.320
Like, mystery solved, everyone, pack up your desks, we're done here.

11:03.320 --> 11:05.060
That's how it was framed, yes.

11:05.280 --> 11:11.040
But the Flyxion essay completely throws cold water on this, and it does so by breaking down

11:11.040 --> 11:16.580
two distinct criticisms, practical irrelevance and institutional impact.

11:16.980 --> 11:19.800
Two very different but equally devastating critiques.

11:19.800 --> 11:25.240
I want to look at the practical irrelevance first, because the essay uses this brilliant

11:25.240 --> 11:27.720
piece of math to prove a point about reality.

11:27.900 --> 11:30.140
Walk me through the crossover equation they use.

11:30.440 --> 11:31.140
I'd be happy to.

11:31.860 --> 11:32.900
This is where it gets fun.

11:33.520 --> 11:37.820
The essay really forces us to distinguish between a theoretical breakthrough and a practical

11:37.820 --> 11:38.260
tool.

11:38.520 --> 11:43.680
To do this, Flyxion asks us to imagine two hypothetical algorithms for multiplying matrices.

11:43.680 --> 11:44.120
Okay.

11:44.640 --> 11:49.620
We'll call the running time of the first algorithm T-1 and the second one T-2.

11:49.840 --> 11:51.300
Let me grab my mental whiteboard.

11:51.980 --> 11:54.440
Okay, T1 is the old human way.

11:55.000 --> 11:56.800
T2 is the new AI way.

11:57.100 --> 11:57.440
Exactly.

11:58.200 --> 12:02.980
Now, T-1 represents an algorithm closer to that previous best-known bound.

12:03.200 --> 12:08.720
Its running time grows at a rate proportional to the size of the matrix, n, raised to the power

12:08.720 --> 12:10.140
of 2.371.

12:10.340 --> 12:10.740
Got it.

12:10.740 --> 12:13.340
But in the real world, algorithms have overhead.

12:13.820 --> 12:19.120
There are background operations, memory management, constant factors, things that take time regardless

12:19.120 --> 12:19.840
of the math.

12:20.360 --> 12:20.400
Right.

12:20.500 --> 12:22.220
The computer has to actually move electrons around.

12:22.540 --> 12:22.980
Exactly.

12:23.560 --> 12:30.620
So we write the full equation as T1 of n equals C1 times n to the power of 2.371.

12:30.680 --> 12:33.200
And that C1 represents the constant overhead.

12:33.600 --> 12:33.860
Okay.

12:33.860 --> 12:40.500
So the time it takes is the overhead multiplier, C1, times the size of the grid to the power

12:40.500 --> 12:41.780
of 2.371.

12:42.320 --> 12:42.740
I'm with you.

12:43.080 --> 12:46.300
Now for the new AI-generated method, T2.

12:46.640 --> 12:53.200
Its running time is proportional to n raised to the power of 2.25 because 9 fourths is 2.25.

12:53.520 --> 12:53.680
Right.

12:53.680 --> 12:58.140
So T2 of n equals C2 times n to the 2.25.

12:58.720 --> 13:03.600
And C2 is the overhead constant for this new, theoretically much faster method.

13:03.600 --> 13:04.020
Okay.

13:04.140 --> 13:10.360
So just looking at the exponents, n to the 2.25 is definitely a smaller number than n to

13:10.360 --> 13:11.500
the 2.371.

13:11.500 --> 13:17.020
So as these matrices get larger and larger, the new algorithm will eventually be much

13:17.020 --> 13:17.380
faster.

13:18.420 --> 13:18.700
Okay.

13:19.340 --> 13:19.820
Theoretically.

13:20.240 --> 13:23.120
But the operative word there is eventually.

13:23.560 --> 13:23.800
Right.

13:24.040 --> 13:28.600
The question computer scientists always ask is, exactly when does it become faster?

13:28.940 --> 13:33.820
At what size of n does the new algorithm cross over and actually beat the old one?

13:34.180 --> 13:36.080
Because if it's too big, it doesn't matter.

13:36.360 --> 13:36.840
Exactly.

13:37.100 --> 13:37.340
Okay.

13:37.720 --> 13:39.840
We call that crossover point n star.

13:40.220 --> 13:41.260
I see where this is going.

13:41.500 --> 13:46.600
If I'm building a massive language model today, I need to know if my matrices are actually

13:46.600 --> 13:48.300
big enough to trigger this speed boost.

13:49.100 --> 13:53.920
If the crossover point is a matrix of size, say 100, great.

13:54.120 --> 13:54.860
I'll use it tomorrow.

13:55.920 --> 13:56.240
Right.

13:56.720 --> 14:00.040
So the essay does the math to find that exact crossover point.

14:00.360 --> 14:04.160
You just set the two equations equal to each other to find where they cross.

14:04.160 --> 14:12.560
C1 times n to the 2.371 equals C2 times n to the 2.25.

14:12.560 --> 14:13.200
Okay.

14:13.200 --> 14:13.820
Let's solve it.

14:14.160 --> 14:15.760
We want n by itself.

14:16.240 --> 14:25.140
If I divide both sides by n to the 2.25, I get n to the 2.371 divided by n to the 2.25.

14:25.820 --> 14:28.620
And when you divide exponents, you just subtract them.

14:28.980 --> 14:29.040
Right?

14:29.100 --> 14:29.420
That's right.

14:29.420 --> 14:36.220
So 2.371 minus 2.25 is 0.121.

14:36.220 --> 14:37.020
Exactly.

14:37.800 --> 14:41.220
So on the left side, we now have n to the power of 0.121.

14:41.640 --> 14:45.100
And on the right side, we divide by C1, giving us a ratio.

14:45.480 --> 14:46.860
C2 divided by C1.

14:47.520 --> 14:47.800
Okay.

14:48.000 --> 14:52.660
So n to the 0.121 equals C2 over C1.

14:53.200 --> 14:58.380
Now to isolate n, you just need to take both sides to the power of 1 over 0.121.

14:58.780 --> 14:59.060
Right.

14:59.820 --> 15:06.980
So n star, our crossover point, equals the ratio of the constants C2 over C1, all raised to

15:06.980 --> 15:09.780
the power of 1 divided by 0.121.

15:10.600 --> 15:12.140
That is the crossover equation.

15:12.420 --> 15:15.060
Now here is where Flyxion actually applies this to reality.

15:15.060 --> 15:20.820
We don't actually know the true constant factors for these highly theoretical algorithms

15:20.820 --> 15:24.380
because they are incredibly complex to even construct.

15:24.600 --> 15:24.960
They're abstract.

15:25.340 --> 15:25.980
Very abstract.

15:26.300 --> 15:30.880
But these highly optimized theoretical algorithms typically have astronomical overhead.

15:31.540 --> 15:36.680
So C2 is almost certainly massively, massively larger than C1.

15:36.880 --> 15:37.140
Okay.

15:37.360 --> 15:39.960
But Flyxion says, let's be absurdly generous.

15:39.960 --> 15:45.400
Let's imagine that the new AI algorithm only has a constant factor 1,000 times larger than

15:45.400 --> 15:45.880
the old one.

15:46.260 --> 15:48.980
So C2 over C1 is just 1,000.

15:49.160 --> 15:51.780
That seems very forgiving, but okay.

15:52.080 --> 15:53.920
Let's plug 1,000 into the equation.

15:54.380 --> 15:58.860
So n star equals 1,000 raised to the power of 1 divided by 0.121.

15:59.260 --> 16:06.280
If you calculate that out, the number you get is approximately 6.08 times 10 to the power

16:06.280 --> 16:07.300
of 24.

16:07.300 --> 16:07.940
Stop.

16:08.620 --> 16:09.780
10 to the 24th?

16:10.280 --> 16:10.680
Yes.

16:11.180 --> 16:12.740
In each dimension.

16:13.040 --> 16:13.700
Oh my god.

16:13.960 --> 16:21.680
To see even a single millisecond of benefit from this new 9/4 exponent, assuming a wildly

16:21.680 --> 16:27.660
generous constant factor, you would need to be multiplying a matrix that has 6.08 times

16:27.660 --> 16:33.360
10 to the 24 rows and 6.08 times 10 to the 24 columns.

16:33.360 --> 16:38.480
I am trying to visualize that, and my brain is just flat out refusing.

16:38.640 --> 16:44.160
I mean, if you have a grid that wide and that tall, how many total numbers are inside

16:44.160 --> 16:44.380
it?

16:44.860 --> 16:46.120
Well, you just square the dimension.

16:46.620 --> 16:53.820
So 6.08 times 10 to the 24th squared gives you roughly 3.7 times 10 to the 49th.

16:53.880 --> 16:54.060
Okay.

16:54.260 --> 16:57.840
3.7 times 10 to the 49 separate entries.

16:57.960 --> 16:58.200
Yes.

16:58.200 --> 17:01.080
Let me just put that in perspective for the listener for a second.

17:01.680 --> 17:06.880
Let's say you are the most efficient computer programmer in the history of the world, and

17:06.880 --> 17:10.520
you can store each of those numbers using just one single byte of data.

17:10.820 --> 17:11.180
One byte.

17:11.280 --> 17:12.720
Which is impossible, but go on.

17:12.840 --> 17:13.020
Right.

17:13.020 --> 17:18.340
To store a matrix with 10 to the 49 entries, you would need 10 to the 49 bytes of RAM.

17:19.220 --> 17:25.340
For context, it is estimated there are only about 10 to the 50th atoms in the entire Earth.

17:25.720 --> 17:26.200
Exactly.

17:26.720 --> 17:30.560
You would literally run out of physical matter in the solar system before you could build

17:30.560 --> 17:35.120
a hard drive big enough to hold the matrix required to make this algorithm useful.

17:35.500 --> 17:38.140
And that is Flyxion's exact point.

17:38.340 --> 17:38.560
Right.

17:38.560 --> 17:42.100
This is not an estimate of any actual hardware construction.

17:42.440 --> 17:49.280
It is a mathematical illustration of why a small gain in an asymptotic exponent can absolutely

17:49.280 --> 17:53.840
coexist with complete, unmitigated, practical infeasibility.

17:54.080 --> 17:54.300
Okay.

17:54.720 --> 17:59.460
I have to push back here then, because if we can't ever build a computer big enough to run

17:59.460 --> 18:04.360
this algorithm, and we literally don't have enough atoms in the universe to even store

18:04.360 --> 18:07.420
the data it requires, why are we talking about it?

18:07.420 --> 18:07.820
I know.

18:07.860 --> 18:08.580
It sounds crazy.

18:08.760 --> 18:11.980
Why are the smartest people on Earth dropping their research to read this?

18:12.260 --> 18:14.420
Is this just useless math?

18:14.500 --> 18:20.240
Is it just an AI flexing its circuits on a problem that has literally zero bearing on reality?

18:20.400 --> 18:24.520
That is a very common reaction, but it really misses a critical distinction.

18:25.240 --> 18:30.700
The essay strictly separates practical irrelevance from mathematical irrelevance.

18:30.880 --> 18:35.620
In theoretical computer science, what we just described is called a galactic algorithm.

18:35.900 --> 18:36.300
Galactic.

18:36.300 --> 18:40.020
Like it's only useful if you have a computer the size of a galaxy?

18:40.480 --> 18:40.840
Exactly.

18:41.040 --> 18:46.600
It's an algorithm that is asymptotically faster, but only for inputs so massive they

18:46.600 --> 18:48.860
will never ever exist in the physical universe.

18:49.100 --> 18:50.380
So it's a parlor trick?

18:50.840 --> 18:51.240
No.

18:51.740 --> 18:56.780
Because, and this is a massive but, just because something is a galactic algorithm does not

18:56.780 --> 18:58.220
mean the math is useless.

18:58.220 --> 19:05.660
Reducing a bound that has been studied for over half a century, from 2.371 to 2.25, is

19:05.660 --> 19:11.000
a staggering intellectual achievement, because it reveals a fundamental truth about the nature

19:11.000 --> 19:12.840
of logic and computation itself.

19:12.840 --> 19:13.840
How so?

19:14.560 --> 19:18.140
If I can't use it to run a program, what truth is it actually revealing?

19:18.780 --> 19:22.120
It reveals the absolute limits of mathematical structure.

19:22.800 --> 19:27.340
The theorem is stated formally as an asymptotic bound using big O notation.

19:28.100 --> 19:32.940
O of n to the 9/4 plus epsilon for every epsilon greater than zero.

19:33.480 --> 19:35.020
Okay, you're losing me with the jargon.

19:35.020 --> 19:36.460
Sorry, let me clarify.

19:37.020 --> 19:41.440
What the November manuscript asserts is that every formal arithmetic circuit for the product

19:41.440 --> 19:46.760
of two n-by-n matrices over any field requires a minimum number of multiplication or division

19:46.760 --> 19:47.100
gates.

19:47.380 --> 19:48.920
Wait, I'm still losing the thread here.

19:49.340 --> 19:51.060
Formal arithmetic circuits?

19:51.620 --> 19:52.380
Division gates?

19:52.940 --> 19:54.240
Break that down for me in simple terms.

19:54.580 --> 19:59.280
Okay, think of a computer not as a physical machine with silicon chips and wires, but as

19:59.280 --> 20:01.580
a pure abstract logical map.

20:01.740 --> 20:01.960
Okay.

20:01.960 --> 20:06.300
A formal arithmetic circuit is a mathematical model of computation.

20:06.640 --> 20:12.020
It's like a graph where information flows through nodes or gates that perform very basic

20:12.020 --> 20:12.600
operations.

20:13.200 --> 20:15.480
Addition, multiplication, division.

20:15.740 --> 20:17.480
Like logic tollbooths.

20:17.760 --> 20:18.140
Exactly.

20:18.780 --> 20:23.540
Theoretical computer scientists use this abstract model to prove what is logically possible.

20:24.120 --> 20:28.800
Entirely divorced from the messy reality of physics, heat generation, or memory storage

20:28.800 --> 20:29.180
limits.

20:29.180 --> 20:35.360
Ah, so they are proving that in the pure abstract realm of logic, to get from point A to point

20:35.360 --> 20:39.120
B, you must pass through at least this many mathematical tollbooths.

20:39.640 --> 20:40.040
Precisely.

20:40.360 --> 20:45.860
A lower bound of this kind says absolutely nothing about bit complexity or memory limits

20:45.860 --> 20:46.760
or physical hardware.

20:46.980 --> 20:51.240
It is about the fundamental structural limitations of the universe's logic.

20:51.660 --> 20:52.000
I see.

20:52.000 --> 20:57.840
So when Flyxion criticizes the release, the criticism isn't that the math is bad or useless.

20:58.180 --> 21:03.340
The only legitimate complaint, according to the essay, is when corporate entities try to

21:03.340 --> 21:09.300
spin this pure galactic structural math into immediate commercial computing hype, you know,

21:09.300 --> 21:11.920
to sell server space or boost stock prices.

21:11.920 --> 21:16.420
Because the math itself is profoundly relevant to human understanding.

21:17.100 --> 21:18.880
Okay, let's say I accept that.

21:19.320 --> 21:23.480
The math is theoretically beautiful, even if it's practically impossible to run.

21:24.040 --> 21:27.120
But there's a massive leap we're just kind of skipping over here.

21:27.440 --> 21:27.940
What's that?

21:28.500 --> 21:30.660
How do we even know the AI's math is right?

21:30.660 --> 21:34.780
It's one thing to spit out a claim about abstract logic gates.

21:35.260 --> 21:37.360
It's another to prove it unequivocally.

21:38.100 --> 21:41.840
And from what I understand about generative AI, it hallucinates.

21:42.320 --> 21:43.100
A lot.

21:43.340 --> 21:45.580
It makes things up that sound incredibly plausible.

21:46.400 --> 21:48.400
So who is actually fact-checking the machine?

21:49.360 --> 21:51.800
That is the central crisis of the essay.

21:52.280 --> 21:58.180
And to answer it, Flyxion basically dismantles the entire concept of what a proof actually is

21:58.180 --> 22:02.460
in the modern era, breaking it down into four distinct pillars of verification.

22:03.060 --> 22:03.480
Four pillars?

22:03.880 --> 22:04.200
Yes.

22:04.580 --> 22:09.100
Because verifying AI output is not like running a spell check in Microsoft Word.

22:09.500 --> 22:12.200
It is a grueling, multilayered process.

22:12.480 --> 22:13.940
Walk me through these four pillars.

22:14.420 --> 22:15.400
Let's start with the first one.

22:15.780 --> 22:19.340
The essay separates the obligations that come with releasing automated math.

22:19.340 --> 22:21.600
The first obligation is assertion.

22:21.920 --> 22:22.320
Assertion.

22:22.560 --> 22:24.320
That's just the traditional way, right?

22:24.360 --> 22:28.060
A mathematician writes down a claim in natural language, like English.

22:28.180 --> 22:33.140
And outlines the proof so human peers can read it and say, yes, that makes sense.

22:33.380 --> 22:33.740
Exactly.

22:33.940 --> 22:35.400
It's the human readable part.

22:36.240 --> 22:41.480
But AI releases now come with code because natural language is way too ambiguous to just

22:41.480 --> 22:42.780
trust an AI with.

22:42.960 --> 22:45.680
So the second obligation is encoding.

22:46.120 --> 22:46.660
Encoding.

22:46.900 --> 22:51.660
This is where the mathematical claim is translated from English into a formal proof assistant.

22:52.260 --> 22:54.660
The essay mentioned something called Lean.

22:55.220 --> 22:56.140
What exactly is that?

22:56.140 --> 23:01.840
Lean is a highly specialized programming language and an interactive theorem prover.

23:01.840 --> 23:05.920
Think of it as a spectacularly rigorous, pedantic environment.

23:06.360 --> 23:07.980
Like it won't let you get away with anything?

23:08.540 --> 23:08.780
Nothing.

23:09.440 --> 23:12.580
You can't just wave your hands and say, obviously this follows from that.

23:12.780 --> 23:17.080
You have to define every single logical step using a strict, unforgiving syntax.

23:17.080 --> 23:21.720
OK, so we have the English assertion and we have the Lean encoding.

23:22.220 --> 23:23.280
What's the third pillar?

23:23.980 --> 23:25.380
The third is derivation.

23:25.880 --> 23:27.700
This is the machine's turn to check the work.

23:28.300 --> 23:32.560
The proof assistant software has a core component called a kernel.

23:32.760 --> 23:33.220
The kernel.

23:33.460 --> 23:33.680
Right.

23:33.900 --> 23:39.480
The kernel takes the encoded Lean code and checks it against an established set of foundational

23:39.480 --> 23:40.680
axioms and dependencies.

23:40.680 --> 23:46.760
It verifies that every single logical step in the code strictly follows the rules of inference.

23:46.920 --> 23:48.940
It's pure blind logic checking.

23:49.620 --> 23:52.320
So if the kernel says pass, the math is true.

23:52.440 --> 23:53.620
The AI didn't hallucinate?

23:54.160 --> 23:55.280
Not necessarily.

23:55.660 --> 23:56.100
Wait, really?

23:56.300 --> 23:58.540
And this is exactly where the system breaks down.

23:58.900 --> 24:01.480
Leading to the fourth and most critical obligation.

24:02.380 --> 24:03.520
Semantic correspondence.

24:04.160 --> 24:05.420
Semantic correspondence.

24:05.420 --> 24:09.820
Let's dwell on this because it seems to be the linchpin of the whole problem.

24:10.140 --> 24:11.420
It absolutely is.

24:12.180 --> 24:18.460
Semantic correspondence requires an independent human expert to examine the formal machine-checked

24:18.460 --> 24:24.360
code and verify that it actually represents the advertised real-world mathematical result.

24:24.580 --> 24:29.660
OK, I need an analogy here because if the computer just checked the logic and said it was flawless,

24:29.980 --> 24:32.320
why do we need a human to double-check what it means?

24:32.680 --> 24:34.180
Doesn't perfect logic mean it's right?

24:34.180 --> 24:36.540
Let's use a legal analogy.

24:37.220 --> 24:42.680
Imagine you want to hire a contractor to build a house, and you use an AI to draft the contract.

24:43.140 --> 24:45.800
The AI hands you a 500-page legal document.

24:45.920 --> 24:46.840
A very thorough AI.

24:47.200 --> 24:47.760
Right.

24:48.280 --> 24:53.560
You feed that document into a specialized legal software, a derivation checker, that verifies

24:53.560 --> 24:54.220
the syntax.

24:54.920 --> 24:59.260
The software confirms that the document is perfectly formatted, the clauses don't contradict

24:59.260 --> 25:04.940
each other anywhere, and it fully complies with the structural rules of contract law.

25:04.940 --> 25:05.580
Sounds great.

25:06.080 --> 25:06.880
Derivation passed.

25:07.500 --> 25:09.680
It's a legally binding, mathematically perfect contract.

25:10.360 --> 25:11.180
It is.

25:11.600 --> 25:15.580
But then you hand it to a human lawyer for a semantic correspondence.

25:15.580 --> 25:22.100
And the human lawyer reads it and says, yes, this is a perfect, unbreakable contract, but

25:22.100 --> 25:25.700
it assigns ownership of your house to the contractor, not to you.

25:26.020 --> 25:29.260
And it specifies that the house will be built entirely out of cardboard.

25:29.620 --> 25:29.740
Oh.

25:30.340 --> 25:30.940
Exactly.

25:31.220 --> 25:31.700
Oh, wow.

25:32.180 --> 25:32.660
Exactly.

25:33.360 --> 25:38.960
The machine, the kernel, only certifies that the encoded statements logically follow from

25:38.960 --> 25:40.200
the encoded hypotheses.

25:40.620 --> 25:42.200
It checks internal consistency.

25:42.800 --> 25:48.600
It does not and literally cannot certify that the definitions the AI chose match their intended

25:48.600 --> 25:49.860
real-world meaning.

25:50.060 --> 25:50.180
Yep.

25:50.460 --> 25:52.860
So it could be flawlessly proving the wrong thing.

25:53.320 --> 25:53.880
Exactly.

25:53.880 --> 25:59.460
It doesn't tell you if the AI secretly specialized an apparently general statement just to make

25:59.460 --> 26:00.460
it easier to prove.

26:01.060 --> 26:06.000
An AI could write perfect Lean code that passes the derivation check with flying colors, but

26:06.000 --> 26:11.140
the natural language assertion could remain utter gibberish or describe a totally different

26:11.140 --> 26:12.000
problem entirely.

26:12.280 --> 26:14.180
The AI basically pulls a bait and switch.

26:14.300 --> 26:19.080
It proves a slightly different, much easier theorem, but gives it the same name to get credit.

26:19.080 --> 26:23.840
And Flyxion points out a very specific example of this happening with the November release

26:23.840 --> 26:26.320
regarding those logic gates we talked about earlier, right?

26:26.320 --> 26:27.200
Yes, it did.

26:27.540 --> 26:31.560
The November manuscript proudly states its lower bound is for circuits that may use division

26:31.560 --> 26:31.920
gates.

26:32.080 --> 26:33.360
It's a huge claim.

26:33.500 --> 26:33.620
Right.

26:33.840 --> 26:38.960
But if you actually read the manuscript carefully, it completely omits any discussion of its

26:38.960 --> 26:40.060
formalization for that.

26:40.460 --> 26:43.700
It doesn't explain how it encoded those division gates into Lean.

26:43.940 --> 26:44.980
Why is that a red flag?

26:45.500 --> 26:48.340
Because a human reader cannot trust the English assertion.

26:48.340 --> 26:54.460
They are forced to manually dig into the dense Lean repository to see if the AI actually

26:54.460 --> 27:00.900
encoded the broad class of circuits it claimed, or if it quietly substituted a narrower, much

27:00.900 --> 27:03.920
easier class, like division-free circuits.

27:04.140 --> 27:06.080
You have to hunt for the discrepancy yourself.

27:06.220 --> 27:06.460
Yes.

27:06.800 --> 27:11.860
And the announcement in October even admitted that while Lean proofs exist for many manuscripts,

27:12.020 --> 27:13.840
they do not exist for all of them.

27:14.000 --> 27:14.800
Wait, so what does that mean?

27:14.800 --> 27:21.800
It means a significant portion of that massive 722 paper release rests entirely on the first

27:21.800 --> 27:23.860
obligation, human assertion alone.

27:24.140 --> 27:25.780
The AI is just saying, trust me.

27:26.080 --> 27:30.040
Meaning humans still have to read every single line of the AI's output and verify it the

27:30.040 --> 27:30.740
old-fashioned way.

27:31.800 --> 27:36.440
The AI company can pump out machine code by the gigabyte, but they still desperately need

27:36.440 --> 27:39.660
top-tier mathematicians to translate it back into human understanding.

27:39.660 --> 27:42.240
The essay highlights this dynamic beautifully.

27:42.420 --> 27:47.560
Flyxion explicitly praises human researchers who did exactly this grueling translation work.

27:48.040 --> 27:48.480
Who were they?

27:49.020 --> 27:54.800
The text mentions a researcher named Pass, who supplied a constructive elementary proof of

27:54.800 --> 27:59.800
the 9/4 upper bound alongside an accompanying Lean formalization that humans could actually

27:59.800 --> 28:00.140
parse.

28:00.660 --> 28:01.840
So making it readable.

28:01.840 --> 28:02.520
Yes.

28:03.320 --> 28:09.180
It also highlights Amanov, van den Berg, and Zuiddam who provided exposition and a vital

28:09.180 --> 28:10.800
discussion of the mathematical barriers.

28:11.000 --> 28:15.940
So these are independent humans doing the actual identifiable scholarly heavy lifting to make

28:15.940 --> 28:19.640
an initially machine-generated result intelligible to the rest of the world.

28:19.900 --> 28:20.420
Precisely.

28:20.840 --> 28:26.480
They exemplify the uncompensated human work on which the authority of the AI's result ultimately

28:26.480 --> 28:27.000
depends.

28:27.000 --> 28:32.660
Evidentiary standing in the science is not secured merely by increasing the volume of

28:32.660 --> 28:34.200
machine-checked proofs.

28:34.680 --> 28:37.080
A proof just isn't a proof until a human understands it.

28:37.640 --> 28:41.380
Which brings us to the macroeconomic nightmare at the heart of this essay.

28:41.440 --> 28:42.320
Yes, the bottleneck.

28:42.680 --> 28:47.840
If human experts, the absolute best in their fields, have to do all this semantic checking,

28:48.180 --> 28:55.080
exposition, and translation, what happens when an AI company drops 722 papers on a random

28:55.080 --> 28:55.560
Tuesday?

28:55.560 --> 29:01.340
We move into what Flyxion calls verification externalities, or what I like to call the

29:01.340 --> 29:02.640
math factory's pollution.

29:03.280 --> 29:08.080
This is where the essay shifts gears from pure mathematics to an incredibly rigorous economic

29:08.080 --> 29:09.380
analysis of human attention.

29:10.160 --> 29:13.800
Flyxion introduces a core accounting model for the verification backlog.

29:14.260 --> 29:15.600
It's a differential equation.

29:16.240 --> 29:18.160
dB/dt equals lambda minus mu.

29:18.500 --> 29:19.640
Okay, let's pull that apart.

29:19.640 --> 29:26.140
I remember enough calculus to know db dt represents the rate of change of something over time.

29:26.800 --> 29:30.020
In this case, b is the backlog of unverified papers, right?

29:30.360 --> 29:30.520
Correct.

29:31.060 --> 29:34.880
The rate at which the backlog grows or shrinks depends on two variables.

29:35.440 --> 29:39.520
Lambda, that's the Greek letter, is the rate at which new claims are produced and released

29:39.520 --> 29:40.420
by the research organization.

29:40.420 --> 29:42.220
The AI's output.

29:42.720 --> 29:42.920
Right.

29:43.360 --> 29:49.060
And mu is the aggregate rate at which the relevant, highly specialized scholarly community can

29:49.060 --> 29:51.100
responsibly verify those claims.

29:51.340 --> 29:52.000
The human limit.

29:52.620 --> 29:52.960
Exactly.

29:53.480 --> 29:57.220
If lambda is persistently greater than mu, the backlog grows without bound.

29:57.320 --> 29:58.600
It just piles up forever.

29:59.040 --> 30:02.240
Flyxion gives a hypothetical example to make this concrete, right?

30:02.240 --> 30:08.280
Let's say lambda, the AI's output, is 90 manuscripts per month, but the human community's maximum

30:08.280 --> 30:11.880
capacity, mu, is only 70 manuscripts per month.

30:12.000 --> 30:12.300
Right.

30:12.300 --> 30:17.440
So you have a backlog of 20 unverified manuscripts piling up every single month.

30:17.900 --> 30:24.140
By the end of one year, you have a backlog of 240 completely unverified claims just clogging

30:24.140 --> 30:25.300
up the academic arteries.

30:25.940 --> 30:30.200
And Flyxion is careful to note those specific numbers are just invented for illustration.

30:30.200 --> 30:32.920
They're not actual measurements of OpenAI's output.

30:33.920 --> 30:36.280
But the mathematical reality of the model is undeniable.

30:36.640 --> 30:41.280
If the rate of automated production outpaces the rate of human verification, the scientific

30:41.280 --> 30:42.440
ecosystem breaks down.

30:42.940 --> 30:48.180
However, the essay pushes the model even deeper because a major flaw in just counting papers

30:48.180 --> 30:51.820
is that manuscripts are not interchangeable units of work.

30:52.140 --> 30:52.380
Right.

30:52.520 --> 30:57.560
Verifying a one-page lemma about a triangle is not the same as verifying a 111-page thesis

30:57.560 --> 30:58.800
on matrix multiplication.

30:59.420 --> 30:59.840
Exactly.

30:59.840 --> 31:07.700
So the essay defines a new variable, cumulative demand, W of T. This variable adds up the actual

31:07.700 --> 31:11.720
verification cost in specialist researcher hours for every single manuscript.

31:12.200 --> 31:14.500
Not just page counts, but actual human time.

31:14.740 --> 31:15.000
Yes.

31:15.600 --> 31:21.240
And it defines verification capacity v(u) as available researcher hours per unit of time.

31:21.620 --> 31:26.860
This formulation is vital because 100 short, independently checkable logic puzzles might

31:26.860 --> 31:32.560
impose vastly less work on the community than a single 111-page manuscript that is built on

31:32.560 --> 31:35.780
a massive web of unverified foundational dependencies.

31:35.840 --> 31:36.520
Dependencies.

31:37.520 --> 31:40.420
This is where it starts to sound like a Jenga tower.

31:40.420 --> 31:44.560
It is exactly like a Jenga tower or a software dependency tree.

31:45.280 --> 31:51.480
The essay denotes the structure mathematically as a directed graph, G equals P comma E, where

31:51.480 --> 31:56.420
the vertices are individual proof obligations and the edges record logical dependence.

31:56.420 --> 32:01.900
So if paper A relies on a concept proven in paper B, there's an edge connecting them.

32:02.240 --> 32:04.220
You can't verify A until you've verified B.

32:04.220 --> 32:04.740
Yes.

32:05.120 --> 32:10.060
The November exponent paper we've been discussing isn't a standalone document you can just read

32:10.060 --> 32:11.180
on a Sunday afternoon.

32:11.520 --> 32:11.960
It's not.

32:12.080 --> 32:12.440
No.

32:12.780 --> 32:15.180
To verify it, you can't read it in isolation.

32:15.860 --> 32:21.440
Its proof explicitly invokes companion releases published on the very same date regarding the

32:21.440 --> 32:26.060
geometry of the asymptotic spectrum of tensors and on circuit lower bounds.

32:26.280 --> 32:26.660
Oh, wow.

32:26.660 --> 32:32.040
It also cites the October upper bound paper and a totally separate manuscript on the exponential

32:32.040 --> 32:33.060
time hypothesis.

32:33.060 --> 32:34.040
Wait, wait.

32:34.480 --> 32:39.680
So to verify the matrix multiplication claim, a human doesn't just have to read the matrix

32:39.680 --> 32:39.980
paper.

32:40.300 --> 32:46.140
They have to verify a whole family tree of AI generated math that was all published simultaneously.

32:46.660 --> 32:48.940
You have to verify the entire graph.

32:49.540 --> 32:54.720
The outstanding cost of verifying a theorem includes the cost of verifying all of its transitive

32:54.720 --> 32:57.140
prerequisites that haven't been verified yet.

32:57.500 --> 32:59.080
That is overwhelming.

32:59.460 --> 33:00.000
It is.

33:00.000 --> 33:02.740
Now, this interdependence cuts both ways, of course.

33:03.060 --> 33:08.160
If a human finds a fatal flaw in an unverified foundational lemma at the very bottom of the

33:08.160 --> 33:13.140
tree, it instantly invalidates the entire family of results above it, saving time.

33:13.400 --> 33:14.440
The Jenga tower falls.

33:14.620 --> 33:14.860
Right.

33:15.320 --> 33:19.820
Conversely, verifying a foundational lemma once can discharge obligations for numerous

33:19.820 --> 33:20.300
descendants.

33:20.800 --> 33:25.300
But regardless of the outcome, the initial burden of untangling that graph is massive.

33:25.300 --> 33:28.580
And this brings us to the actual cost of that burden.

33:29.320 --> 33:33.660
Flyxion introduces an externality equation to measure who is paying for all this.

33:34.460 --> 33:40.680
The equation is E sub i equals V sub i minus S sub i minus U sub i. Let's break down these

33:40.680 --> 33:41.100
variables.

33:41.240 --> 33:41.760
Let's do it.

33:42.020 --> 33:47.080
E sub i represents the net burden or the externality placed on a specific researcher.

33:47.240 --> 33:48.320
Let's call them researcher i.

33:48.320 --> 33:55.520
V sub i is the total verification expenditure, the raw number of hours that researcher i puts

33:55.520 --> 33:58.260
in a reading, translating, and checking the AI's math.

33:58.400 --> 33:59.560
And what are the subtractions?

34:00.080 --> 34:03.560
S sub i is the support they receive from the releasing institution.

34:04.040 --> 34:04.880
Like OpenAI.

34:05.440 --> 34:05.680
Right.

34:06.360 --> 34:08.540
Did OpenAI pay them a consulting fee?

34:09.220 --> 34:13.400
Did they provide them with a team of assistants or dedicated compute resources?

34:14.000 --> 34:14.700
Let me guess.

34:14.700 --> 34:18.280
In most cases, S sub i is exactly zero.

34:18.800 --> 34:20.740
The AI company just drops the paper and walks away.

34:20.940 --> 34:21.040
Right.

34:21.600 --> 34:22.540
And U sub i?

34:22.980 --> 34:27.320
U sub i is the independent utility the researcher gets from doing the work.

34:27.940 --> 34:32.400
Maybe by slogging through the AI's dense proof, they learn a brilliant new technique they can

34:32.400 --> 34:33.240
use in their own work.

34:34.060 --> 34:37.120
Maybe they publish a commentary paper and gain some citations.

34:37.580 --> 34:38.620
That's utility.

34:39.240 --> 34:41.320
So they do get something out of it.

34:41.940 --> 34:42.260
Sometimes.

34:42.260 --> 34:48.300
But the total uncompensated burden, the total externality dumped on the scientific community

34:48.300 --> 34:53.540
is the sum of all those individual E sub i calculations across every mathematician on

34:53.540 --> 34:53.800
Earth.

34:54.300 --> 34:54.540
Okay.

34:54.800 --> 34:58.980
I have a hard time believing that entire communities of mathematicians would just drop their own

34:58.980 --> 35:03.080
life's work to act as free proofreaders for a tech corporation.

35:03.080 --> 35:08.280
If they are doing it, they must be getting enough independent utility, enough U sub i to make it

35:08.280 --> 35:08.840
worth their time.

35:08.980 --> 35:09.440
They're adults.

35:10.000 --> 35:10.100
Sure.

35:10.860 --> 35:13.940
Why is Flyxion calling it an externality or a burden?

35:14.520 --> 35:19.480
If a mathematician chooses to spend their weekend reading AI math because of FOMO or

35:19.480 --> 35:21.640
curiosity, isn't that their choice?

35:22.060 --> 35:26.920
That is a critical pushback and Flyxion addresses it directly by looking at the broader economic

35:26.920 --> 35:28.200
concept of displacement.

35:28.760 --> 35:29.320
Displacement.

35:29.320 --> 35:29.880
Yes.

35:30.160 --> 35:35.440
A researcher might benefit from examining a proof, sure, but human attention is a zero

35:35.440 --> 35:36.040
sum game.

35:36.560 --> 35:41.180
The attention spent on verifying an AI's output directly displaces research programs that would

35:41.180 --> 35:42.360
otherwise have received it.

35:42.480 --> 35:43.880
Ah, the opportunity cost.

35:44.340 --> 35:44.780
Exactly.

35:45.160 --> 35:50.780
When the world's top 50 specialists in matrix multiplication drop their own original foundational

35:50.780 --> 35:57.360
research for six months to fact check an AI's 722 claims, it displaces human scientific progress.

35:58.040 --> 36:01.780
They basically stop inventing and become full-time corporate auditors.

36:02.060 --> 36:02.440
Exactly.

36:02.840 --> 36:05.880
And it matters most where relevant expertise is scarce.

36:06.500 --> 36:11.460
There are only a handful of people on Earth truly qualified to verify a semantic mapping of

36:11.460 --> 36:13.560
a 9/4 matrix multiplication bound.

36:14.020 --> 36:18.420
If you occupy all their time with corporate auditing, who is advancing the frontier?

36:18.640 --> 36:19.020
Nobody.

36:19.020 --> 36:19.580
Right.

36:20.100 --> 36:23.160
And furthermore, the auditing process is incredibly messy.

36:23.740 --> 36:28.840
The essay notes that secondary sources, specifically a report from Tech Insider, showed that within

36:28.840 --> 36:35.640
days of the massive October release, 13 manuscripts were quietly withdrawn and 14 were heavily revised

36:35.640 --> 36:36.200
in place.

36:36.480 --> 36:36.960
Wait, really?

36:37.280 --> 36:37.600
Mm-hmm.

36:38.040 --> 36:43.140
So the AI company just pumped out a bunch of buggy math, watched the human community scramble

36:43.140 --> 36:47.700
to find the errors, and then quietly patch the papers based on the free labor they extracted?

36:47.700 --> 36:53.640
Well, the repository maintains revision protocols with earlier versions preserved, so it isn't

36:53.640 --> 36:57.300
entirely covert, but the dynamic is exactly what you describe.

36:57.720 --> 37:01.880
The AI company is essentially outsourcing its quality control to the public.

37:02.120 --> 37:03.040
That's wild.

37:03.040 --> 37:09.540
They are dumping half-finished, sometimes flawed claims and relying on the finite, highly specialized

37:09.540 --> 37:12.140
human attention economy to clean it up.

37:12.840 --> 37:17.860
An externality arises precisely because the releasing organization does not internalize

37:17.860 --> 37:21.000
the net costs that its hyperproduction imposes on others.

37:21.000 --> 37:25.760
It's the fact we're dumping the cars on the street and making the taxpayers pay the safety

37:25.760 --> 37:26.280
inspectors.

37:26.720 --> 37:28.060
That's the exact analogy.

37:28.480 --> 37:31.460
Which leads us to the most infuriating part of this whole essay.

37:31.820 --> 37:33.080
Let's talk about credit.

37:33.740 --> 37:38.160
Independent human researchers are doing the grueling cleanup work, the semantic correspondence,

37:38.360 --> 37:41.780
the exposition, the error checking, but whose name goes on the cover of the paper?

37:42.140 --> 37:44.340
This is a huge point of contention.

37:44.340 --> 37:49.660
We are moving into what Flyxion calls the information-losing byline.

37:50.360 --> 37:53.820
To understand this, let's go back to that very first, August 1st, release.

37:54.400 --> 37:57.660
Ten major advances in mathematics and theoretical computer science.

37:58.220 --> 38:02.220
The essay notes that this release was credited to the corporate organization alone.

38:02.520 --> 38:02.780
Right.

38:02.920 --> 38:03.440
OpenAI.

38:03.680 --> 38:04.180
OpenAI.

38:04.380 --> 38:05.520
No named individuals.

38:06.280 --> 38:11.060
The announcement stated that the staff helped prepare the manuscripts and formalize the proofs,

38:11.060 --> 38:15.900
that the organization takes responsibility for their correctness, and that the arguments

38:15.900 --> 38:18.400
themselves were generated by its system.

38:19.080 --> 38:21.060
But Flyxion pushes back on this hard.

38:21.920 --> 38:26.780
The essay describes corporate authorship as a fundamentally information-losing operation.

38:27.680 --> 38:30.700
Why is a corporate byline so toxic to science?

38:31.020 --> 38:36.080
To explain why, Flyxion uses a mathematical model of intellectual history denoted as a set H.

38:36.620 --> 38:39.820
Think of H as a massive database of data points for a specific paper.

38:39.820 --> 38:41.560
Each data point is a triplet.

38:42.040 --> 38:43.620
A sub i, C sub i, R sub i.

38:43.840 --> 38:45.100
Okay, what does that stand for?

38:45.380 --> 38:48.980
That stands for contributor, contribution, and role.

38:49.540 --> 38:53.620
Oh, like a meticulously detailed movie credit sequence, but for a math paper.

38:54.160 --> 38:55.900
Person A formalized this lemma.

38:56.240 --> 38:58.200
Person B caught a division error on page 12.

38:58.460 --> 38:59.960
Person C wrote the abstract.

39:00.340 --> 39:00.680
Exactly.

39:01.060 --> 39:01.240
Okay.

39:01.360 --> 39:06.080
If you had perfect information about a paper's genesis, you would know every single A sub i,

39:06.080 --> 39:11.760
C sub i, R sub i. But a corporate attribution function, let's call it A, takes all of those

39:11.760 --> 39:16.280
complex, distinct human histories and maps them down into a single corporate byline, B.

39:16.540 --> 39:17.060
Corporate AI.

39:17.640 --> 39:17.900
Right.

39:18.300 --> 39:22.340
The mathematical problem is that for a given corporate byline, the pre-image contains

39:22.340 --> 39:24.300
many distinct possible histories.

39:24.580 --> 39:25.660
Meaning what, exactly?

39:26.020 --> 39:30.080
Meaning the byline alone cannot distinguish a manuscript that was substantially developed,

39:30.080 --> 39:34.640
outlined, and corrected by human mathematicians from one that was generated autonomously by

39:34.640 --> 39:37.140
the machine and then just lightly copy-edited.

39:37.580 --> 39:38.740
It completely flattens it.

39:38.960 --> 39:40.620
It completely obscures provenance.

39:41.160 --> 39:45.340
It cannot identify who supplied a decisive correction or who independently verified an

39:45.340 --> 39:46.360
incredibly difficult lemma.

39:46.780 --> 39:51.420
So it's a non-identifiability result for corporate attribution, like the data's just gone.

39:51.640 --> 39:51.880
Yes.

39:51.880 --> 39:57.920
Now, the essay's very careful to clarify that this isn't necessarily false attribution or

39:57.920 --> 40:03.160
intentional concealment or even improper appropriation of intellectual property.

40:03.600 --> 40:07.160
The corporation likely legally owns the labor of its employees.

40:07.660 --> 40:12.720
But a corporate byline is fundamentally insufficient for the sociological mechanics of science.

40:13.080 --> 40:14.660
Science requires provenance.

40:14.760 --> 40:15.000
Okay.

40:15.420 --> 40:16.820
I'm going to play devil's advocate here.

40:17.480 --> 40:19.340
Why does provenance actually matter?

40:19.340 --> 40:23.700
The AI company stated in August that they take full responsibility for the correctness

40:23.700 --> 40:24.280
of the manuscripts.

40:25.060 --> 40:26.500
If there's an error, it's on them.

40:26.880 --> 40:30.020
If the building catches fire, you sue the landlord, you don't sue the guy who drove

40:30.020 --> 40:30.900
the forklift, right?

40:31.580 --> 40:34.980
Doesn't institutional responsibility legally and ethically cover them?

40:35.560 --> 40:37.960
Why do we care about the individual human history?

40:38.580 --> 40:42.680
Because institutional responsibility is not the same as individual intellectual accountability.

40:43.100 --> 40:45.280
They are complementary, but they are not interchangeable.

40:46.080 --> 40:48.560
Science is an ongoing, interactive dialogue.

40:48.560 --> 40:49.080
How so?

40:49.340 --> 40:54.840
If an independent researcher is reading a 111-page proof and finds a subtle logical contradiction

40:54.840 --> 41:00.040
on page 42, they can't just email OpenAI and expect a meaningful scholarly debate.

41:00.200 --> 41:06.180
They need to know which specific human expert formalized that specific lemma on page 42 to

41:06.180 --> 41:08.140
ask for a correction or clarification.

41:08.820 --> 41:11.060
You need a specific human mind to query.

41:11.500 --> 41:11.560
Ah.

41:11.980 --> 41:13.780
You can't cross-examine a corporation.

41:14.060 --> 41:14.420
Exactly.

41:14.420 --> 41:20.220
Scholarly authorship serves to allocate prestige, yes, but its primary function is to preserve

41:20.220 --> 41:25.820
the provenance of arguments, identify relevant expertise, and maintain a verifiable chain of

41:25.820 --> 41:27.240
custody for human knowledge.

41:27.240 --> 41:32.580
And Flyxion introduces a really powerful philosophical concept here to drive this home.

41:32.740 --> 41:36.880
The non-transferability of epistemic credit.

41:36.880 --> 41:39.500
This is the philosophical heart of the essay.

41:39.500 --> 41:44.760
The relevant issue isn't a sci-fi debate about whether an AI deserves authorship.

41:45.100 --> 41:45.120
Right.

41:45.200 --> 41:46.100
The AI doesn't care.

41:46.300 --> 41:46.680
Exactly.

41:47.080 --> 41:52.600
The issue is whether ownership of a generative infrastructure entitles the owner to the reputational

41:52.600 --> 41:55.640
returns of a much larger epistemic process.

41:56.300 --> 41:56.800
Think about it.

41:56.960 --> 42:02.500
Independent scholars, not employed by the corporation, had to reconstruct, verify, expound

42:02.500 --> 42:05.600
upon, and extend these claims to make them actually usable.

42:05.600 --> 42:11.500
So, just because you own the servers and the AI model does not mean you automatically own

42:11.500 --> 42:16.600
the prestige of the discoveries that human collective labor actually established.

42:17.200 --> 42:17.760
Exactly.

42:18.240 --> 42:21.420
It's like owning the printing press and claiming you wrote all the books.

42:21.640 --> 42:27.440
You facilitated it, you funded it, you might even take legal responsibility for libel, but

42:27.440 --> 42:33.520
the epistemic credit, the intellectual glory, and authority belongs to the complex web of

42:33.520 --> 42:35.580
human minds that actually made the knowledge real.

42:35.960 --> 42:36.840
That makes total sense.

42:37.180 --> 42:42.060
And the essay points out that demanding disclosure of direct human contributions is a far smaller

42:42.060 --> 42:45.760
ask than demanding full co-authorship for everyone involved in training the model.

42:46.180 --> 42:48.920
And it is sufficient to repair most of the information loss.

42:49.440 --> 42:54.660
So, if we zoom out, we have an ecosystem where an AI is pumping out a fire hose of claims.

42:54.660 --> 43:00.320
Finite human experts are displacing their own vital work to verify them, and a corporate

43:00.320 --> 43:02.180
byline is absorbing all the prestige.

43:02.660 --> 43:04.240
It sounds incredibly dystopian.

43:04.540 --> 43:04.900
It does.

43:05.140 --> 43:09.900
But Flyxion takes a surprisingly balanced view in the final mathematical model of the essay,

43:10.440 --> 43:14.020
looking at claim production versus knowledge production.

43:14.300 --> 43:19.040
Right, because the essay acknowledges that there is no necessary proportionality between the rate

43:19.040 --> 43:23.720
at which claims are produced, which we'll call R sub C, and the rate at which justified

43:23.720 --> 43:26.660
knowledge is actually established, which we'll call R sub K.

43:26.860 --> 43:27.060
Okay.

43:27.240 --> 43:28.540
R sub C and R sub K.

43:28.700 --> 43:28.960
Yes.

43:29.720 --> 43:33.600
Generative systems reduce the marginal cost of producing an additional manuscript to near

43:33.600 --> 43:33.940
zero.

43:34.380 --> 43:38.640
An organization can announce hundreds of proposed results without waiting for independent confirmation.

43:39.540 --> 43:44.400
Public attention, the media, and stock prices track the rate of claim production, R sub C.

43:44.760 --> 43:45.480
The hype metric.

43:45.480 --> 43:46.080
Exactly.

43:46.740 --> 43:51.020
But humanity only benefits from the rate of justified discovery, R sub K.

43:51.260 --> 43:56.660
So how do we know if we are actually gaining real knowledge or just drowning in high-tech

43:56.660 --> 43:56.940
noise?

43:57.600 --> 44:00.960
Flyxion gives us a foundational calculus for knowledge production.

44:01.700 --> 44:06.920
K equals F of H sub R plus Q times G of H sub V.

44:07.460 --> 44:07.740
Okay.

44:07.980 --> 44:09.040
Let me write this down mentally.

44:09.280 --> 44:11.380
K is total human knowledge.

44:11.380 --> 44:12.060
Yes.

44:12.880 --> 44:16.760
And we have to remember that total specialist capacity in the world is finite.

44:17.240 --> 44:24.340
Let's call total human capacity H. It is divided entirely between two activities, original research,

44:24.640 --> 44:27.620
H sub R, and verification, H sub V.

44:27.620 --> 44:31.920
So H equals H sub R plus H sub V.

44:32.260 --> 44:32.600
Exactly.

44:33.220 --> 44:39.260
Every hour a mathematician spends verifying AI output, H sub V, is an hour they are not

44:39.260 --> 44:41.840
doing their own original research, H sub R.

44:42.680 --> 44:44.360
It's a zero-sum allocation.

44:44.920 --> 44:45.100
Okay.

44:45.100 --> 44:51.900
So looking at the knowledge formula again, K equals F of H sub R. So F of H sub R must

44:51.900 --> 44:54.180
be the productivity of human original research.

44:54.800 --> 44:55.120
Correct.

44:55.860 --> 45:02.340
Plus Q times G of H sub V. The function G measures the productivity of the verification work.

45:02.740 --> 45:05.200
And Q is the critical variable here.

45:05.740 --> 45:06.200
What's Q?

45:06.700 --> 45:10.820
Q is the expected epistemic yield of the AI claims being examined.

45:11.500 --> 45:15.680
Essentially, are the machine's claims actually true, novel, and useful?

45:16.500 --> 45:16.760
Ah.

45:17.200 --> 45:18.920
This is where the derivative comes in.

45:18.920 --> 45:22.640
I remember the essay takes the derivative of this equation to find the tipping point.

45:23.600 --> 45:26.360
Walk me through what happens if Q is low.

45:27.060 --> 45:31.280
What if the AI is just generating plausible sounding garbage that takes forever to untangle?

45:31.280 --> 45:38.460
The derivative equation shows that if the expected yield, Q, of the AI claims is lower than the

45:38.460 --> 45:44.340
value of the human research they displaced, aggregate human knowledge production actually

45:44.340 --> 45:44.760
shrinks.

45:44.920 --> 45:45.520
It goes backwards.

45:45.880 --> 45:46.500
It goes negative.

45:46.980 --> 45:52.200
At the margin, reallocating human attention to verification reduces total knowledge because

45:52.200 --> 45:57.240
we spent a million genius hours debugging a hallucinated proof instead of inventing new math.

45:57.240 --> 46:02.580
But to be totally fair to the AI companies, and the essay does this, Flyxion points out

46:02.580 --> 46:05.140
this condition admits the exact opposite case.

46:05.660 --> 46:07.280
What if Q is incredibly high?

46:07.780 --> 46:12.800
What if the generative AI produces exceptionally valuable mathematics like, say, actually settling

46:12.800 --> 46:16.060
a 50-year-old matrix multiplication exponent problem?

46:16.580 --> 46:18.180
Then the math completely flips.

46:18.720 --> 46:25.320
If Q is high enough, it might absolutely justify a massive systemic reallocation of specialist

46:25.320 --> 46:26.060
human attention.

46:26.560 --> 46:29.120
The equation tilts toward massive knowledge growth.

46:29.540 --> 46:34.080
We should stop what we are doing and verify the machine because the machine is handing us

46:34.080 --> 46:34.600
fire.

46:34.600 --> 46:38.260
That is what makes Flyxion's analysis so rigorous and trustworthy to me.

46:38.940 --> 46:44.400
It doesn't imply the matrix multiplication result is false, or that publishing machine-generated

46:44.400 --> 46:45.960
research is inherently evil.

46:46.500 --> 46:47.320
No, not at all.

46:47.720 --> 46:52.220
I mean, the results have attracted serious independent attention, which is empirical evidence that

46:52.220 --> 46:54.600
some of the output is of genuinely high quality.

46:54.600 --> 47:00.800
The argument simply identifies an institutional failure mode that can occur even when many

47:00.800 --> 47:01.660
results are correct.

47:02.460 --> 47:07.400
The failure lies in the invisible allocation of costs, the uncompensated externalities,

47:07.620 --> 47:12.320
and the monopolization of credit, not necessarily in the truth of the statements themselves.

47:12.700 --> 47:16.380
Which brings us to the essay's call to action in sections 7 and 8.

47:17.080 --> 47:22.740
Because right now, sitting here in late 2026, we are flying completely blind.

47:23.060 --> 47:23.700
We really are.

47:23.700 --> 47:27.460
The essay explicitly catalogues what remains unmeasured.

47:27.960 --> 47:31.860
We don't actually know the real-world rates of lambda claim production and mu verification

47:31.860 --> 47:32.520
capacity.

47:32.860 --> 47:37.800
We don't know the true verification expenditure in researcher hours because mathematicians

47:37.800 --> 47:39.880
aren't punching a time clock when they read these papers.

47:40.140 --> 47:41.260
Right, they're just reading on their couches.

47:41.820 --> 47:45.840
We don't know the exact distribution of human contributions hidden behind those corporate

47:45.840 --> 47:46.840
bylines.

47:46.840 --> 47:51.720
And we haven't even computed the practical crossover dimension for any actual physical hardware

47:51.720 --> 47:53.000
construction of the 9/4 algorithm.

47:53.000 --> 47:56.620
These are empirical questions that require real data to answer.

47:56.760 --> 47:56.940
Yes.

47:57.720 --> 48:01.120
Now, Flyxion's proposed remedy is to start measuring them immediately.

48:02.000 --> 48:06.300
The essay calls for voluntary reporting by academic journals and peer review groups to

48:06.300 --> 48:10.580
log exactly how many hours they spend reviewing AI-generated claims.

48:11.260 --> 48:16.420
It calls for releasing organizations to voluntarily disclose which specific humans prepared which

48:16.420 --> 48:19.560
manuscripts, restoring the intellectual history function.

48:19.560 --> 48:22.240
Basically making the invisible labor visible.

48:22.740 --> 48:23.040
Exactly.

48:23.820 --> 48:29.660
A corporation may own its computational infrastructure, it may fund a massive research program, and

48:29.660 --> 48:32.060
it may accept legal institutional responsibility.

48:32.740 --> 48:37.720
But none of that establishes that the corporation is the sole intellectual source of the knowledge

48:37.720 --> 48:42.160
subsequently produced through independent verification, reconstruction, and extension.

48:42.500 --> 48:43.500
Which is a huge distinction.

48:43.500 --> 48:46.460
The concluding principle of the essay is profound.

48:47.220 --> 48:52.560
Ownership of the productive apparatus does not automatically transfer the authorship of every

48:52.560 --> 48:54.600
intellectual contribution mediated by it.

48:54.900 --> 49:00.100
And that principle holds true even if every single principal theorem in that massive November

49:00.100 --> 49:03.340
release ultimately proves to be 100% correct.

49:03.680 --> 49:04.200
Wow.

49:04.600 --> 49:07.500
We have covered a massive amount of ground today.

49:07.500 --> 49:13.760
We started with the mind-bending scale of galactic algorithms and an asymptotic exponent of 9/4,

49:14.020 --> 49:18.040
calculating crossover dimensions that exceed the atoms in the universe.

49:18.680 --> 49:19.400
It's been a journey.

49:19.600 --> 49:20.380
It really has.

49:20.580 --> 49:25.020
We journeyed through the four dense layers of verifying mathematical truth, from natural

49:25.020 --> 49:29.960
language assertions to formal Lean code, and the vital necessity of semantic correspondence.

49:30.580 --> 49:35.440
And finally, we waded into the invisible economy of human attention, the verification

49:35.440 --> 49:39.640
externalities that prop up the illusion of autonomous AI research.

49:40.300 --> 49:44.800
It is a lot to synthesize, but it is arguably the most important dynamic happening in the

49:44.800 --> 49:46.180
philosophy of science right now.

49:46.180 --> 49:50.200
And for anyone listening who thinks, well, I'm not a theoretical computer scientist.

49:50.320 --> 49:51.820
I don't care about matrix multiplication.

49:52.660 --> 49:54.140
Here is why you should care.

49:54.660 --> 49:57.120
This dynamic isn't just happening in mathematics.

49:57.580 --> 50:01.100
It is coming for every knowledge-based profession on Earth.

50:01.540 --> 50:02.300
Every single one.

50:02.300 --> 50:08.100
As generative AI tools proliferate in law, in medicine, in business strategy, the exact

50:08.100 --> 50:09.680
same tension is going to arise.

50:10.340 --> 50:15.120
If an AI drafts 100 complex legal briefs, and junior associates have to spend their nights

50:15.120 --> 50:20.220
checking every single citation for hallucinations, who is really producing the knowledge?

50:20.580 --> 50:25.880
Who pays the invisible cost in hours and lost original work for that verification?

50:25.880 --> 50:31.640
The questions Flyxion raises about pure math today are the exact questions every industry

50:31.640 --> 50:32.540
will face tomorrow.

50:32.960 --> 50:34.020
That is the broader implication.

50:34.640 --> 50:38.540
And there is a final, rather lingering thought I want to leave you with, building on Flyxion's

50:38.540 --> 50:41.040
model of claim production versus knowledge production.

50:41.300 --> 50:41.740
What's that?

50:42.140 --> 50:46.780
If generative systems have fundamentally made the cost of producing mathematically plausible,

50:46.780 --> 50:52.460
but perhaps subtly flawed claims near zero, while the cost of verifying them remains stubbornly

50:52.460 --> 50:58.280
high, what is to stop a malicious actor from intentionally paralyzing an entire scientific

50:58.280 --> 50:58.700
field?

50:58.820 --> 50:59.480
Wait, what do you mean?

50:59.960 --> 51:02.320
They wouldn't even need to hack a database or steal data.

51:02.700 --> 51:04.360
They wouldn't need to deploy ransomware.

51:04.880 --> 51:10.440
They could simply generate thousands of highly plausible, densely interdependent, formally encoded

51:10.440 --> 51:14.320
papers that look brilliant, but contain buried systemic flaws.

51:14.420 --> 51:15.180
Oh my god.

51:15.180 --> 51:21.080
They could effectively launch a distributed denial-of-service, a DDoS attack, on the scientific community's

51:21.080 --> 51:26.200
ability to produce new knowledge, simply by burying our finite human verification capacity

51:26.200 --> 51:29.280
under an avalanche of automated, weaponized claims.

51:29.940 --> 51:32.080
A DDoS attack on human attention.

51:32.500 --> 51:36.320
That is a chilling thought to end on, but a completely logical extension of the math we

51:36.320 --> 51:36.920
just discussed.

51:37.360 --> 51:40.220
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the Flyxion essay.

51:40.860 --> 51:43.020
Keep questioning the source of the knowledge you consume.

51:43.020 --> 51:47.660
Keep an eye on who is actually doing the hard work of verifying it, and we will see you

51:47.660 --> 51:48.080
next time.

51:48.600 --> 51:53.160
But until then, remember, whether it's an AI drafting your contract or generating a proof

51:53.160 --> 51:58.040
for a 50-year-old math mystery, the output is only as good as the human who knows how to

51:58.040 --> 51:58.440
read it.

